  "client_view_text": " We assume a flush policy that ensures that every committed transaction is safely stored on disk before another transaction can proceed.\n\n## Step 1: Understanding the Write-Ahead Log\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a component of a database's storage system that ensures the integrity of database transactions even in the event of a system crash or failure. It works by keeping a record of all changes made to the database before they are actually written to the database files on disk. This includes the changes made by each transaction.\n\n## Step 2: Transaction Commit Process\nWhen a transaction is committed, the database system records the transaction's changes in the WAL, marking the transaction as committed. This step is crucial because it ensures that if a crash occurs after a transaction is committed but before its changes are written to disk, the database can still recover from the crash by replaying the committed transaction's changes from the WAL.\n\n## Step 3: Crash Recovery Process\nIn the event of a crash, the database system's first step is to recover from the crash by replaying all committed transactions found in the WAL that were not yet written to disk. This process involves redoing the operations listed in the WAL for the transactions that were committed up until the point of the crash. By replaying"
